        <tr><td>$50,001+</td><td>3.876%</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>New York vs. Zero-Tax States: Annual Take-Home Comparison</h2>
  <div class="table-responsive">
    <table class="data-table">
      <thead><tr><th>Location</th><th>Gross</th><th>State + Local Tax</th><th>Est. Annual Net</th><th>vs. FL/TX</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td><strong>NYC Resident</strong></td><td>$100,000</td><td>~$8,650</td><td>~$67,400</td><td style="color:#dc2626;font-weight:700;">−$10,700</td></tr>
        <tr><td><strong>NY State (non-NYC)</strong></td><td>$100,000</td><td>~$5,450</td><td>~$70,600</td><td style="color:#dc2626;font-weight:700;">−$7,500</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Florida / Texas</td><td>$100,000</td><td>$0</td><td>~$78,100</td><td>—</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>How to Reduce Your New York Tax Burden</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Max your 401(k):</strong> At a combined 30%+ marginal rate (federal + NY + NYC), every $1,000 in pre-tax contributions saves $300+ in taxes.</li>
    <li><strong>Contribute to HSA:</strong> Unlike California, New York recognizes HSA deductions at the state level — reducing both federal and NY state taxable income.</li>
    <li><strong>Consider NY 529 Plan:</strong> New York allows up to $5,000 ($10,000 married) deduction for contributions to a NY 529 college savings plan.</li>
    <li><strong>Review your W-4 and IT-2104:</strong> Ensure both federal and NY state withholding forms accurately reflect your situation to avoid over-withholding.</li>
    <li><strong>Evaluate residency:</strong> If you spend significant time outside NYC, you may qualify as a non-resident — potentially avoiding NYC local tax entirely.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
  <% const _faqs = [["What is the New York state income tax rate?", "New York has progressive rates from 4% to 10.9%. The top rate applies to income over $25 million. For typical earners ($50K-$150K), the effective rate is 5-6.5%. NYC residents add 3.08-3.88% local tax on top."], ["How much do NYC residents pay in local tax?", "NYC local income tax ranges from 3.078% to 3.876%. On a $100K salary, this adds approximately $3,200/year in additional taxes beyond NY state tax. Combined state + local can reach 14.8%."], ["How much take-home on $100K in New York?", "NYC resident: ~$67,400/year net. NY state (non-NYC): ~$70,600/year net. Compared to Florida/Texas: ~$78,100/year net."], ["Does New York tax remote workers?", "NY applies the \"convenience of employer\" rule — if your employer is NY-based and you work remotely elsewhere for convenience (not necessity), NY may still tax your income. This is highly controversial and subject to legal challenges."], ["What is the NY standard deduction?", "NY standard deduction is $8,000 (single) / $16,050 (married filing jointly). This is separate from and lower than the federal standard deduction."], ["Is the NY MCTMT taken from my paycheck?", "No. MCTMT is employer-paid. Self-employed individuals in the MTA region earning over $50,000 pay it directly."], ["What is the Yonkers income tax?", "Yonkers residents pay a 16.75% surcharge on their NY state tax liability. Non-residents working in Yonkers pay 0.50% of Yonkers wages."], ["How does NY compare to Florida for take-home?", "On $100K (single, NYC resident), you net ~$67,400 vs. ~$78,100 in Florida — $10,700/year less. Even non-NYC NY workers net ~$7,500/year less than Florida workers."]]; _faqs.forEach(function(f) { %><div class="rc-faq-item"><div class="rc-faq-q"><%- f[0] %></div><div class="rc-faq-a"><%- f[1] %></div></div><% }); %>

